  "summary": "Today's health-tech news highlights three major themes reshaping healthcare delivery and policy. Ambient AI and clinical scribing technologies are gaining traction in hospitals nationwide, with Maine General and rural providers using these tools to improve efficiency, while the FDA is actively developing standards for clinical AI implementation. Regulatory and political currents are shifting around controversial treatments and oversight: an FDA advisory panel narrowly voted to allow compounding pharmacies to produce unapproved peptides like BPC-157 and KPV—a win for RFK Jr.—while separately, advocacy groups are pushing the FDA to ban direct-to-consumer prescription drug advertising, and a Senate nominee to run ASPR faces delays due to vaccine safety concerns. Finally, Congress is advancing several health care bills before August recess, with price transparency and prior authorization reforms topping the agenda, while unrelated developments include a new cyclospora outbreak investigation, supply issues with Lantus Solostar insulin, and mixed cardiovascular data on Eli Lilly's obesity drug retatrutide in heart disease patients.",
